+# Decision: Concurrent edit policy for visit records
+
+## Context
+
+Two field workers can independently edit the same plot-visit record — comparing field notes days apart, often while one or both tablets have been offline for up to 48 hours. The record store must decide what a visit's current record is when competing edits arrive. The stakes are scientific: an overwriting policy silently loses an ecologist's observation, and an automatic combination can silently cross scientific relationships between fields (a species identification and its cover estimate were estimated together; treating them as independent values can produce a record no one observed). Both siblings that consume this contract — tablet-sync, which submits edits, and review-export, which produces the Friday CSV for the regional science partner — size their designs around this outcome.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **No silent loss of a submitted edit.** Every accepted submission must remain retrievable regardless of the policy's automatic outcome. This is the HLD tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and eliminates any policy whose automatic path can overwrite a peer's edit without a human seeing it.
+- **Operable by two coordinators without a dedicated systems administrator.** This is the HLD tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*; it eliminates policies whose correctness depends on ongoing curation the team cannot staff.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Preservation of whole-visit intent** (major; tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*). A visit's fields were observed as a unit; a revision should stand or fall as that unit.
+- **Merge-rule maintenance burden** (major). Any rule the server applies to scientific data must be written, understood, and kept current by people who understand the science; every rule is a place where wrong behavior is silent.
+- **Coordinator review volume** (moderate). Two coordinators resolve the queue; the policy must not generate more review than they can clear weekly before the Friday export.
+- **Submission size and tablet complexity** (minor at current scale; payloads are small structured records, tablets are commodity hardware).
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each edit names the revision it was made against (its base) and submits the complete visit payload. Concurrent edits to the same starting state produce competing complete revisions that fork the visit's history; the server combines nothing and holds the visit unresolved until a coordinator resolves the branch, either by selecting one revision as the continuation or by recording a reconciled revision containing the coordinator's combined payload. Original submissions are immutable and permanently retained.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A corrects an observer name from revision R3; Worker B adds a missing species also from R3. Both revisions exist with base R3. The visit is unresolved; a coordinator opens the two revisions side by side and either picks A's (losing B's species addition as current, though retained) or records a reconciled revision with the corrected name *and* the added species.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- No silent loss: **passes.** All revisions retained; resolution is always a human choice.
+- Coordinator operability: **passes.** Comparing two complete visits is a reading task, not a configuration task.
+- Whole-visit intent: **strong.** A revision is exactly what a worker submitted — the scientific unit is preserved even under conflict.
+- Merge-rule burden: **strong.** There are no merge rules to maintain; the server applies no interpretation to field values.
+- Coordinator review volume: **weak.** Every same-base concurrency conflict becomes review work, including edits to unrelated fields that a human would trivially combine.
+- Submission size / tablet complexity: **partial.** Tablets resend the full payload and must track a base revision ID; trivial at this data size.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a larger review queue for zero server-side interpretation of scientific data.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each edit supplies its base state and a structured set of field changes. The server applies changes per field: changes to disjoint fields are combined automatically into a merged revision; changes touching the same field are the only thing sent to review. Requires a maintained rule set distinguishing field kinds — scalar fields, observer lists, species cover entries — with list-merge semantics (add, modify, remove per entry). Field-level provenance is retained so any merged value can be traced to its contributing edit.
+
+**Demonstration.** Same scenario: A's observer-name change and B's species addition touch disjoint fields, so the server combines them automatically and the visit never enters review. Only if both revised the same species entry's cover estimate would a coordinator see work.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- No silent loss: **passes** narrowly — edits are retained with provenance, but the *automatic combination* can still be wrong without anyone seeing it (below), which is loss of a different kind: loss of the record someone actually observed.
+- Coordinator operability: **passes**, but the merge-rule set is an ongoing administrative surface these two coordinators inherit.
+- Whole-visit intent: **weak.** Combination decomposes the visit into independent field values, exactly the decomposition the science does not support — a revised identification and its cover estimate can be combined into a state no observer held.
+- Merge-rule burden: **eliminated by criterion, not gate — strong negative.** Rules per field kind must be authored and kept current; a wrong or stale rule produces silent miscombinations, the failure mode this project exists to prevent.
+- Coordinator review volume: **strong.** Only overlapping edits reach review; far fewer unresolved visits.
+- Submission size / tablet complexity: **partial-to-weak.** Tablets must track base snapshots and diff into structured changes — more client state and a larger envelope.
+
+**Summary.** Trades maintained merge rules and whole-visit decomposition for a much smaller review queue.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions** is selected. The decision turns on the *merge-rule burden* and *whole-visit intent* criteria, both major: the project's founding failure was silent data change during spreadsheet import, and field-aware merging reintroduces a variant of it — a rule combining "scientifically related" edits without noticing is precisely the silent change this system must not make. The gate *no silent loss of a submitted edit* technically passes under both options, which is why the decision is criterion-driven, not gate-driven. The cost — a larger coordinator review queue (weak on review volume) — is accepted deliberately: two coordinators resolving side-by-side revisions is visible, correctable work, whereas a wrong automatic merge is invisible until the science partner's CSV is wrong.
+
+The field-aware option remains credible and is not foreclosed forever: if review volume from concurrent same-base edits outgrows the two-coordinator capacity in a future season, the reconciliation layer can be revisited — the retained base revisions already carry everything a three-way merge would need (base state, competing payloads), so the options compose in that order rather than exclude each other. What is foreclosed now is automatic combination without human review.
+
+**Downstream commitments this decision obligates:**
+
+- *tablet-sync* must retain the base revision ID of the state each local draft was made against, submit complete visit payloads (not diffs), handle a submit outcome of "unresolved — competing revisions pending," and surface that state to field workers without implying their edit was lost or rejected.
+- *review-export* must provide a revision comparison view for unresolved visits (side-by-side payloads with authors and receipt times), hold unresolved visits out of the Friday CSV (export eligibility = linear revision chains only), and resolve through the record store's Resolve operation — never by direct record mutation.
+- Both siblings must take the unresolved-work representation from the record store's contract rather than deriving their own concurrency semantics.
