-## Architecture
+## Revision Model
 
-The record store exposes authenticated submission, current-state retrieval, submission-history retrieval, and review-outcome operations. Persistence keeps original submissions, their authors, and server receipt times. A submission carries an idempotency key so a connection failure does not create another edit on retry.
+Every accepted submission becomes an immutable **revision** of its visit. A revision carries:
 
-A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names.
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| revision ID | Server-assigned, immutable, unique per visit |
+| visit UUID | The client-generated visit identifier |
+| base revision ID | The revision the edit was made against; null for a visit's initial submission |
+| visit payload | Complete visit: plot UUID, observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes |
+| author ID | Authenticated submitting worker |
+| server receipt time | When the server accepted it |
+| idempotency key | Client-generated; makes retries safe |
 
-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+Revisions are never mutated or deleted. Two accepted revisions naming the same base revision are **competing revisions**: independent edits of the same starting state that the server does not attempt to combine.
+
+## Current State and Unresolved Visits
+
+A visit's revisions form a tree rooted at the initial submission, linked by base revision ID. The visit's state follows from the tree's shape:
+
+- **Linear** — the revisions form a single chain. The chain tip is the **current record**, and the visit is exportable from this node's perspective.
+- **Branched** — some base revision has more than one accepted child. The visit is **unresolved**: it has no current record, and it stays unresolved until a coordinator resolves the branch. Arrival order never breaks a tie.
+
+**Resolution.** A coordinator resolves a branched visit by either:
+
+- **Selecting** one competing revision as the continuation, or
+- **Recording a reconciled revision** — a new revision whose base is the branched revision's base, containing the coordinator's combined payload.
+
+The selected or reconciled revision becomes the new chain tip. Revisions not on the winning chain are retained permanently and remain visible in submission history, but can never become current except through a later resolution that selects them.
+
+**Submission against a non-tip base.** A submission naming a base that already has another accepted child is accepted and creates a new branch — the visit becomes (or remains) unresolved. A submission naming a base that is off the winning chain (superseded by a resolution) is accepted under the same rule; the coordinator sees it as new competing work rather than the server rejecting field work outright.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references against the catalog before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload exactly as submitted, separately from any coordinator-combined representation. A retry with the same idempotency key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+
+Retrieval distinguishes usable current state from unresolved review work. The unresolved-work representation exposes, per unresolved visit: the branched base revision ID and the competing revision IDs, each with author, receipt time, and payload — enough for review-export to render a side-by-side comparison without a second call.
+
+## Interface
+
+| Operation | Who | Behavior |
+|---|---|---|
+| Submit revision | Field worker (via tablet-sync) | Creates a revision; returns revision ID and the visit's resulting state (linear tip or unresolved) |
+| Get visit state | Any caller | Returns the current record, or an unresolved marker with the competing revision IDs |
+| List revisions | Any caller | Full submission history: every revision with author, receipt time, base revision ID, and chain status (current / on-chain superseded / off-chain) |
+| Resolve | Coordinator | Records resolution outcome: coordinator identity, selected revision ID or reconciled payload, and contributing revision IDs |
+
+Chain status is derived from the revision tree at read time, not stored per revision, so resolutions and later submissions never require rewriting history rows.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy for concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions: edits name a base revision; competing same-base edits fork the tree and go to coordinator review | Field-aware three-way merge (disjoint field changes auto-combine, overlaps go to review); server-authoritative last-write-wins | Last-write-wins silently discards a submitted edit, violating the HLD tenet. Field-aware merge reduces review volume but requires maintained merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and automatic combination can silently cross scientific relationships (e.g., a revised species identification and its cover estimate edited independently) that the rules do not understand. Whole-visit revisions preserve each worker's submitted visit as a scientific unit and push all conflicts to a human, accepting a larger review queue as the cost. See docs/intent/record-store/decisions/concurrent-edit-policy.md |
+| Competing revisions are never auto-merged by the server | Server combines nothing | Auto-combine edits that touch disjoint fields | Auto-combination is the field-aware mechanism the team declined; mixing a weaker version of it into whole-visit revisions would reintroduce the risk the choice was made to avoid, while splitting the review model in two |
+| Branch status derived at read time | Compute chain status from the revision tree on read | Store a mutable status column per revision | Stored status must be rewritten on every resolution and later submission; derivation keeps revisions immutable and cannot go stale |
+| Off-chain submissions are accepted, not rejected | Accept and surface as new competing work | Reject submissions whose base was superseded by a resolution | Rejecting turns a server state change into a field blocker; workers may be offline for 48 hours and cannot rebase interactively. The coordinator, not the server, judges whether the late edit still matters |
+
+## Downstream Obligations (cascade notes)
+
+These obligations fall out of decisions made in this segment; the sibling nodes own their designs.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+- **tablet-sync**: retains the base revision ID of the state it edits against, submits complete visit payloads, and displays unresolved outcomes (competing revisions pending) to field workers.
+- **review-export**: renders a revision comparison view for unresolved visits; holds unresolved visits out of the exported CSV; resolves via the Resolve operation defined here.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+### Resolved
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy: whole-visit revisions — docs/intent/record-store/decisions/concurrent-edit-policy.md
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+### Deferred
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+1. Whether the Resolve operation accepts a reconciled payload directly or only a selection among existing revisions, pending the review-export interface design.
+2. Resolution of the edge cases raised in the LLD edge-case probe (retained or deferred per user triage).
 
